    answer: "Rowing center design is the engineering of boathouses and waterfront training facilities — long, narrow boat bays for 60-plus-foot shells, waterfront docks and launches, and the training, locker, and ergometer rooms that support a rowing program. The engineering splits in two: the civil and waterfront work (dredging, bulkheads, floating docks, shoreline stabilization, floodplain compliance) and the building itself (clear-span boat storage, high-bay doors, washdown and drainage, and humidity control to protect expensive shells). Rowing centers live at the water's edge, which means every project negotiates flood zones, environmental permits, and shoreline regulations alongside the architecture. I've worked on waterfront buildings, and the pattern is consistent — the permitting and the water-side engineering take longer and cost more than anyone expects, while the boathouse itself is a straightforward long-span building if the program is clear.",
    directAnswer: "Rowing center design is the engineering of boathouses, docks, and waterfront training facilities for rowing programs. It covers waterfront civil engineering (docks, bulkheads, dredging, shoreline permits), clear-span boat storage for long shells, and the washdown, drainage, and humidity control that protect boats and keep the facility working year-round.",
