        body: "Rowing center design is the engineering of boathouses, docks, and waterfront training facilities for rowing programs. It covers waterfront civil engineering (docks, bulkheads, dredging, shoreline permits), clear-span boat storage for long shells, and the washdown, drainage, and humidity control that protect boats and keep the facility working year-round.\\n\\nThe waterfront is the project. Before a single boat bay is drawn, the civil engineer is establishing the shoreline condition, flood elevation, permit pathway, and dock configuration — because those constraints fix the building's location, elevation, and orientation. A boathouse designed without the waterfront resolved is a building looking for a site; a boathouse designed from the water inward is a facility that actually works.",
