    h1: "What Engineering Goes Into a Competitive Fencing Center?",
    answer: "Fencing center design is the engineering of dedicated halls for Olympic fencing — long strips (pistes) of 14 meters each, specialized conductive flooring or piste surfaces, and the lighting, scoring infrastructure, and viewing areas of a fencing salle. The engineering is about the strip: fencers need long, narrow, perfectly flat lanes with the right surface conductivity for electronic scoring, and the lighting has to be bright and even without creating glare on masks or blinding fencers during attacks. The floor system needs grip for explosive lunges and enough resilience for a sport played entirely on the feet. HVAC serves a dense, high-exertion hall quietly and without drafts (a foil is light, and fencers notice air movement). It's a linear, disciplined building type — the hall is essentially a machine for parallel strips.",
    directAnswer: "Fencing center design is the engineering of dedicated salles with regulation pistes, scoring infrastructure, and lunge-tuned flooring. It covers long clear-span strip layouts, conductive piste surfaces and electronic scoring systems, glare-free even lighting, and the quiet draft-free HVAC fencers need.",
